+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    The versions to sort. If omitted, versions are read from *standard in*,
+    one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text, one
+version per line, which makes the output easy to read in a shell loop::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ pysemver sort 2.0.0 1.0.0 | while read v; do echo "release $v"; done
+    release 1.0.0
+    release 2.0.0
+
+Versions can also be piped in::
+
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+If there are no versions (no arguments and empty input), nothing is printed.
+If any version is invalid,
+an error message is printed and the return code is 2.
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    The versions to consider. If omitted, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The result is printed on *standard out* as JSON: a string, or ``null``
+if there is no version that is not a prerelease (including no input).
+Unlike :command:`pysemver sort`, the output is meant to be parsed
+(for example with ``fromJSON()``)::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.2.0 2.0.0-rc.1 1.10.0
+    "1.10.0"
+    $ printf '1.0.0-rc.1\n' | pysemver latest
+    null
+
+If any version is invalid,
+an error message is printed and the return code is 2.
+
+
 Return Code
 -----------
 
